WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.700
Very likely you're gonna
make quite a few mistakes.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:07.140
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here, and
today I want to talk about something

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.980
a little different as it relates to
language learning and that is reward

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.960
yourself in your language learning.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.000
It's gonna help you remember.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.520
If you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.610
Click on the bell for more
uh, notifications and if you

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.610
follow me on a podcast service,
uh, please leave a comment.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.060
So the biggest problem that people
have in language learning is that they

00:00:30.105 --> 00:00:33.915
give up, they get frustrated, they
don't feel that they're making any

00:00:33.915 --> 00:00:40.435
progress, and they stop continuing to
listen and read and study the language.

00:00:40.905 --> 00:00:45.465
And I think there are a number of things
that we can do to maintain our motivation,

00:00:46.035 --> 00:00:48.765
but one of them is to reward yourself.

00:00:48.795 --> 00:00:52.215
Like I decided to go back to Turkish.

00:00:52.215 --> 00:00:54.465
So I was listening to
Turkish while out for a walk.

00:00:54.975 --> 00:00:59.985
And while I've now forgotten a lot
of my Turkish I recognized that I

00:00:59.985 --> 00:01:02.444
hear the words very, very clearly.

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:06.285
I don't know necessarily what
the words mean, but I hear them.

00:01:06.705 --> 00:01:10.664
So in any, whenever we're learning a
language, when we're at a stage where

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:16.995
we hear the words clearly, where word
ends and the next word begins, and,

00:01:16.995 --> 00:01:20.054
and we kind of notice that word very
clearly, we just have to look it up.

00:01:20.054 --> 00:01:22.095
We don't know what it means, or
we've forgotten what it means,

00:01:23.104 --> 00:01:24.450
I give myself credit for that.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.710
I say, yeah, wow.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:29.370
You know, it's gonna be fun
relearning Turkish because

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.110
I hear the words so clearly.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.970
I have mentioned before that we shouldn't,
you know, get upset when we forget things

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:40.740
or when we, um, are unable to say things
or when we don't understand things.

00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:44.850
It, it's far better to reward ourselves
for the things that we are able to do.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.740
And, and that's something that we are able
to do, should be something that's a little

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:54.390
difficult to do 'casue it's a, if it's a
little difficult to do and we can reward

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:57.360
ourselves, the brain actually likes that.

00:01:57.389 --> 00:02:02.100
Apparently there's, you know, research
that talks about this being, you

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.149
know, in the zone and attempting to do
something that's a little difficult,

00:02:06.149 --> 00:02:08.350
but actually being able to carry if off.

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:10.920
We have that sense of
achievement, we are encouraged.

00:02:10.925 --> 00:02:11.660
The brain is...

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:15.800
gets, I don't know, a shot of endorphins
or whatever and wants to do more of that.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.200
So it's important to reward yourself.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:23.180
Unfortunately, traditional
language instruction works more

00:02:23.185 --> 00:02:25.460
on the principle of penalizing us.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:29.300
So we have a test, we're gonna
test what was just taught.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.410
The teacher's gonna test you.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.530
Very likely you're gonna
make quite a few mistake.

00:02:34.109 --> 00:02:37.560
As you're doing this test, you're
almost reluctant to answer the

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:40.740
question because there's a high
likelihood you're gonna get it wrong.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.510
That's one thing, by the way that I like
about the matching pairs that we have

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.560
now in the new version that I'm testing
on iOS, and which will eventually be

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.880
available on all our platforms, that
you can get it wrong and very quickly,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.519
you know, okay oh, that one isn't right.

00:02:56.519 --> 00:03:00.209
So, You know, maybe these
are the pairs that match.

00:03:00.329 --> 00:03:04.109
In other words, do things in a
way that you're easily rewarded

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:08.130
and not so easily knocked down.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.920
Because I think maintaining our motivation
is so important, and we have to bear

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.450
in mind that the process of learning a
language is one of, it's a slow process.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.480
First of all, we shouldn't have
expectations that we're gonna learn

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:25.960
anything very quickly, and it's sort
of like peeling layers of an onion or

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:28.320
I've compared it to a jigsaw puzzle.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:32.580
Slowly, slowly things become,
you know, clearer to us.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:38.010
So if at first, I, I hear the divisions,
you know, where a word ends and where

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.310
the next word begins, but I don't know
what the words mean but still, I can

00:03:41.315 --> 00:03:45.180
accept that as a step in the right
direction, wherever it's possible to

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.490
say, good, you know, you did that.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:52.799
That's why we have our statistics, so
that when we're in that long period

00:03:52.804 --> 00:03:57.000
where we don't have the impression that
we're improving, at least the statistics

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.570
tell me that I've been active and I've
created LingQs and I've read so many

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:05.340
words, and I've listened to so many
hours because I know personally that

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.850
if I am active, then I will learn.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:12.880
That's the advantage of this idea
of having a challenge where you're

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.489
trying to maintain your streak.

00:04:14.929 --> 00:04:19.110
Because even though you may not feel
you've improved that day, at least if you

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:21.900
maintained your streak, you did something.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.790
You're rewarding yourself
for your level of activity.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.220
Because even though learning a
language is a slow process, if

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:33.090
you stay with it, if you remain
active, you are going to improve.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:37.349
Even though very often you don't
really feel that you're improving.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:42.750
So my, the main message today is
reward yourself for what you can do.

00:04:42.989 --> 00:04:46.979
Don't criticize yourself for
what you're not capable of doing.

00:04:47.159 --> 00:04:51.840
There will be many moments when you
perhaps disappoint yourself and you

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:55.770
shouldn't, you know, emphasize that
sort of disappointment or frustration.

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:57.299
You should give your credit.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:02.329
Give yourself credit for things that
you are doing, whether it is hearing

00:05:02.329 --> 00:05:06.255
something, whether it is, you know,
if you're studying Russian and you

00:05:06.255 --> 00:05:11.505
notice more and more, you're seeing the
letter that we think is P acts as an

00:05:11.505 --> 00:05:13.665
R, you know, in the Cyrillic alphabet.

00:05:14.085 --> 00:05:18.165
All of these things that seem
frustratingly difficult to do, not

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:20.655
because they're difficult, but because
our brain isn't getting used to it.

00:05:21.315 --> 00:05:25.065
And wherever we have an opportunity
to recognize that we have stepped

00:05:25.065 --> 00:05:30.434
forward, taken a step forward, or
at least that our, uh, statistics at

00:05:30.434 --> 00:05:34.155
LingQ show us that we have been active,
we should say, we should accept the

00:05:34.155 --> 00:05:36.645
credit for that you're doing well.

00:05:36.914 --> 00:05:41.240
And this might sound sort of
silly, you know, how very often

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.660
now in kids sporting events, they
want to give everybody a prize.

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:46.910
It's not a matter of
giving everybody a prize.

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:49.910
Obviously if you have a race, somebody
came first, second, and third.

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:51.620
But language learning isn't a race.

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:55.820
Language learning is a long,
uh, long distance run where

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:58.520
you have to develop stamina.

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:03.320
And one thing that's gonna feed that
stamina is giving yourself credit

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:07.190
for what you're doing, what you're
achieving, and how active you are.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:14.670
Okay, so, so let's have a look at how
these statistics might be motivating.

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:19.530
So I decided to put a
little effort into Turkish.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:24.120
So over the last three days I've
been doing quite a bit of listening.

00:06:24.719 --> 00:06:30.599
So I may not have the sense that I've
necessarily improved a lot, but I see

00:06:30.599 --> 00:06:34.710
that I've put a lot of effort into
listening in the language and I reminding

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.780
myself of this level of activity.

00:06:37.094 --> 00:06:43.034
It's rewarding if I check, uh, you
know, words of reading, I'll also see

00:06:43.034 --> 00:06:46.755
that I've read, you know, 1,800 words.

00:06:47.325 --> 00:06:51.885
Um, this is, you know, you need
to remind yourself that being

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:53.565
active is gonna get you there.

00:06:53.565 --> 00:06:58.094
Obviously if I look at, you know,
this month, I'll see that all this

00:06:58.094 --> 00:07:03.195
activities in the last few days, but
if I get back to the last seven days,

00:07:03.675 --> 00:07:07.875
and if I look at um LingQs created.

00:07:07.935 --> 00:07:12.164
I've also been active, so
it is just a minor thing.

00:07:12.405 --> 00:07:18.855
You know, my coins, I'm exceeding my
daily, albeit a very modest daily target.

00:07:19.335 --> 00:07:24.045
Uh, I have on occasion when I've
been in a, a challenge, I've set

00:07:24.284 --> 00:07:25.815
a much more demanding target.

00:07:25.815 --> 00:07:28.545
I've set a target of,
let's say, 400 coins.

00:07:28.545 --> 00:07:28.664
A.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:31.080
Uh, which is then tough.

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:33.120
You've gotta work to, to, to hit that.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:35.250
The a hundred coins is very easy to hit.

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:38.100
You know, create a few
links, a few LingQs, listen a

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:39.960
little bit, and you're there.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:44.880
So, uh, just to show you that
there are ways you can use, uh,

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:48.360
your statistics at LingQ to remind
yourself that you are being active.

00:07:48.659 --> 00:07:52.380
And if you believe, and you
should believe that if you're

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:56.280
active, you're gonna improve, then
that should be, uh, motivating.

00:07:58.005 --> 00:08:02.115
And, uh, before I end the video,
I thought I would direct you to

00:08:02.115 --> 00:08:05.594
a couple of videos that I did on
related subjects not so long ago.

00:08:06.075 --> 00:08:11.115
Uh, one talks about motivation,
uh, how to stay motivated, and the

00:08:11.115 --> 00:08:13.305
other talks about frustrations.

00:08:13.575 --> 00:08:16.965
And obviously if we can reward
ourselves, we can stay motivated

00:08:16.965 --> 00:08:18.795
and we can avoid frustration.

00:08:19.125 --> 00:08:20.025
Thank you for listening.

00:08:20.085 --> 00:08:20.505
Bye for now.

